      "hints": [
        "Use cos²(5x)=(1+cos(10x))/2 before integrating.",
        "Find an exact antiderivative of both the constant and cosine terms, keeping the factor 3.",
        "Apply the limits π/5 and π/4 using exact sine values, then simplify without decimals."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Use the double-angle formula $\\cos^2\\theta = \\dfrac{1 + \\cos(2\\theta)}{2}$ with $\\theta = 5x$:\n$$\\cos^2(5x) = \\dfrac{1 + \\cos(10x)}{2}$$\n$$3\\cos^2(5x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}(1 + \\cos(10x)) = \\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{3}{2}\\cos(10x)$$",
        "Integrate term-by-term with respect to $x$:\n$$\\int \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{3}{2}\\cos(10x)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\left[ \\dfrac{3}{2}x + \\dfrac{3}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin(10x)}{10}\\right) \\right] = \\left[ \\dfrac{3}{2}x + \\dfrac{3}{20}\\sin(10x) \\right]$$",
        "Evaluate at the upper limit $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$:\n$$\\dfrac{3}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\dfrac{3}{20}\\sin\\left(10 \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{8} + \\dfrac{3}{20}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{2}\\right)$$\n$$\\text{Since } \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\pi + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1:$$\n$$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{8} + \\dfrac{3}{20}(1) = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{8} + \\dfrac{3}{20}$$",
        "Evaluate at the lower limit $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$:\n$$\\dfrac{3}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right) + \\dfrac{3}{20}\\sin\\left(10 \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10} + \\dfrac{3}{20}\\sin(2\\pi)$$\n$$\\text{Since } \\sin(2\\pi) = 0:$$\n$$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10} + 0 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}$$",
        "Subtract the lower limit value from the upper limit value:\n$$\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{8} + \\dfrac{3}{20}\\right) - \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{3}{20} + \\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{8} - \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}\\right)$$\n$$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{8} - \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{15\\pi - 12\\pi}{40} = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{40}$$\n$$\\text{Exact value} = \\dfrac{3}{20} + \\dfrac{3\\pi}{40}$$"
      ]
    }
  ]
